UID:f0b657f3c578e3fe@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261022T213350Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor secures court order re
 quiring New Jersey employer to pay $700K in back wages\, damages to 48 wor
 kers denied overtime pay
DESCRIPTION:VOORHEES\, NJ – The U.S. Department of Labor has obtained 
 a consent judgment and final order in federal district court requiring a N
 ew Jersey drywall installer to pay $725\,000 in back wages\, damages\, and
  civil money penalties after denying 48 workers overtime pay.The departmen
 t’s Wage and Hour Division found that P & B Partitions Inc. failed to pa
 y their employees for pre-shift work while they waited for transportation 
 to their jobsites. The failure to pay constitutes a violation under the Fa
 ir Labor Standards Act’s overtime provisions.  “Tasks completed to p
 repare for the job\, and in this case\, while waiting to get to the worksi
 te\, is work that must be compensated\,” said Wage and Hour Division Dis
 trict Director Charlene Rachor in Southern New Jersey. “Failing to pay w
 orkers overtime wages deprives them of their rights\, and we will hold emp
 loyers accountable for such actions.”The consent judgment requires P & B
  Partitions Inc. to pay $350\,000 in back wages\, an equal amount in liqui
 dated damages to 48 workers\, and requires payment of an additional $25\,0
 00 in civil penalties. The judgment was entered in the U.S. District Court
  for the District of New Jersey on June 26\, 2026.“The U.S. Department o
 f Labor will pursue legal action to ensure that all employees receive the 
 compensation they are legally owed\,” said Regional Solicitor of Labor J
 effrey S. Rogoff in New York. “This case demonstrates that employers can
  face costly consequences if they do not comply with federal wage law.”E
 mployees and employers can contact the Wage and Hour Division with questio
 ns and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-free helpline\, 86
 6-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged to use the agency’s indu
 stry-specific compliance assistance toolkits to learn about their respon
 sibilities under the laws enforced by the division. The agency’s PAID p
 rogram offers employers an opportunity to self-report and resolve potenti
 al minimum wage and overtime violations under the FLSA\, as well as\nhttp:
 //www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261022
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261022
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2082e537ac2f234f@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261022T213350Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Unemployment Insurance Weekly Claims Report
DESCRIPTION:In the week ending October 17\, the advance figure for seasona
 lly adjusted initial claims was 194\,000\, a decrease of 6\,000 from the p
 revious week's revised level. The previous week's level was revised up by 
 1\,000 from 199\,000 to 200\,000. The 4-week moving average was 197\,750\,
  a decrease of 1\,000 from the previous week's revised average. The previo
 us week's average was revised up by 250 from 198\,500 to 198\,750.\nhttp:/
 /www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261022
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261022
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
