BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d72cd861bee1a5d8@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor awards $95.5M in grant
 s to support innovative programs for reskilling\, reemploying dislocated w
 orkers
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today announced th
 e award of $95.5 million in funding to support the creation of innovativ
 e models of training and employment services that connect workers affected
  by layoffs to high-growth industries.Administered by the department’s 
 Employment and Training Administration\, Rapid Reskill Dislocated Worker G
 rants support 20 projects across 17 states to quickly connect workers affe
 cted by layoffs to employment and training services that lead to high-wage
 \, in-demand occupations in industries expected to grow\, including domest
 ic manufacturing\, aerospace\, energy production\, construction\, and othe
 r occupations important to reindustrialization.Reskill DWG projects are de
 signed to swiftly assess participants’ prior experience and existing ski
 llsets and chart a course to an in-demand occupation or industry. Particip
 ants will gain industry-specific skills and credentials to speed their ree
 mployment while projects will develop and share innovative approaches for 
 connecting workers impacted by layoffs with careers in America’s growth 
 industries.“The Rapid Reskill Grants support our commitment to ensuring 
 displaced workers have the skills they need to pursue the American Dream\,
 ” said Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling. “They also help ensure a w
 orkforce for the Golden Age of reindustrialization and empower workers to 
 stay rooted in their hometown.”Reskill DWGs aim to rapidly place disloca
 ted workers into paid work\, whether full-time unsubsidized employment\, a
  Registered Apprenticeship\, or other paid\, structured on-the-job trainin
 g in their target occupation or industry within six months of enrollment.
  Toward that end\, these innovative projects incorporate short-term\, inte
 nsive training programs that lead to industry-recognized credentials and s
 kills development aligned with industry-specific or occupational training.
  Supported by the Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act\, National Di
 slocated Worker Grants provide funding assistance t\nhttp://www.dol.gov/n
 ewsroom/releases/eta/eta20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261019
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f18aa85c00c3393@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Consent judgment orders California-based home
  appliance company to pay $800K to 339 employees denied overtime pay
DESCRIPTION:LOS ANGELES – The U.S. Department of Labor has obtained a c
 onsent judgment requiring a home appliance company to pay $800\,000 in bac
 k wages and damages to 339 employees after an investigation found the comp
 any violated federal wage laws.The judgment comes after the department’s
  Wage and Hour Division investigated Coway USA Inc. and found that the emp
 loyer paid employees on a piece-rate basis and failed to pay employees for
  time spent attending mandatory meetings\, picking up and loading supplies
 \, and performing work before and after their shifts\, in violation of the
  Fair Labor Standards Act. Investigators also found that Coway did not inc
 lude nondiscretionary incentives\, bonuses\, and commissions in overtime p
 ay calculations and frequently reduced the number of hours employees recor
 ded working on their timecards.Entered in the U.S. District Court for the 
 Central District of California on Sept. 9\, the consent judgment requires 
 Coway to record all hours the implicated employees worked\, including time
  they spend picking up supplies\, attending mandatory training\, and commu
 nicating with customers before and after appointments. Coway is also requi
 red to give employees pay statements that show their hours worked\, servic
 es provided to customers\, and the rate Coway paid for the hours and servi
 ces. The consent judgment orders Coway USA Inc. to pay $400\,000 in unpai
 d overtime wages and an equal amount in damages to 339 employees working i
 n the greater metro areas of Los Angeles\, New York City\, and Washington\
 , D.C. The company also agreed to pay $10\,000 in penalties for willful vi
 olations. Coway USA Inc. is a subsidiary of Coway Co. Ltd.\, a multinatio
 nal company specializing in home appliances. The company installs and main
 tains various appliances\, including water and air filtration products thr
 oughout the U.S.Employers and employees can contact the Wage and Hour Divi
 sion with questions and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-fre
 e helpline\, 866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Emp\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/rel
 eases/whd/whd20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261019
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eecd49abb937f346@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor awards more than $10.5
 M in grants to support mine safety\, health training across the nation
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor announced today t
 hat its Mine Safety and Health Administration has awarded more than $10.5 
 million in grants to reduce mining accidents\, injuries\, and illnesses by
  supporting training and education programs.The agency is awarding these g
 rants to support the delivery of federally mandated training for new and e
 xperienced miners at surface and underground coal and metal and nonmetal m
 ines. MSHA is awarding grants in 45 states\, the Navajo Nation\, and the C
 ommonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands.“Thanks to President Trump
 ’s leadership we’re investing billions of dollars into critical mining
  projects and resources for mining education\,” said Secretary of Labor 
 Keith Sonderling. “Today’s grant announcement furthers the Department
 ’s goal to expand mining across the United States to support this Admini
 stration’s goals to provide critical resources to the workers industries
  that continue to expand in America.”States and territories apply for gr
 ant funding\, which is then awarded to state mine inspectors’ offices\, 
 state departments of labor\, and state-supported colleges and universities
 . Training and education programs are tailored locally to address conditio
 ns and hazards miners may experience.“A strong and thriving America reli
 es on a mining industry that safely produces the minerals essential to our
  economy\,” said Assistant Secretary for Mine Safety and Health Wayne Pa
 lmer. “These grants will promote safe and healthful workplaces for over 
 300\,000 miners across 47 states\, territories\, and tribal lands.”Learn
  more about MSHA.The recipients of MSHA State Grants are as follows: Reci
 pientCityState/TerritoryAwardBevill State Community College JasperAL $24
 3\,711University of Alaska at FairbanksFairbanksAK $160\,886Arizona State
  Mine Inspector’s OfficePhoenixAZ $453\,633Navajo Nation Minerals Depar
 tmentWindow RockAZ $46\,430Arkansas Department of Labor and LicensingLitt
 le Rock AR $138\,903Central Connecticut State UniversityNew BritainCT $
 98\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261019
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:72a48630a55cffee@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor announces field assist
 ance bulletin withdrawing 3 opinion letters on tipped employees
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour
  Division today announced the issuance of a new field assistance bulletin 
 addressing the treatment of related duties performed by tipped employees u
 nder the Fair Labor Standards Act. Field Assistance Bulletin No. 2026-1 
 brings the division’s guidance on tipped employees with dual jobs into a
 lignment with current regulations.The bulletin withdraws the division’s 
 1979\, 1980\, and 1985 opinion letters concerning the application of dual 
 jobs regulations to tipped employees. The withdrawal of the three opinion 
 letters is necessary because they no longer reflect the division’s polic
 y or interpretation of the FLSA. The bulletin also clarifies that the curr
 ent dual jobs regulation focuses on whether an employee performs duties re
 lated to the employee’s tipped occupation\, rather than imposing a speci
 fic percentage-based limit on the time an employee may spend performing re
 lated\, non-tipped duties within that occupation.The publication of the ne
 w bulletin follows a 2024 federal court decision that vacated the departme
 nt’s 2021 dual jobs final rule. After that decision\, the department rem
 oved the regulatory text adopted under the 2021 rule and reinstated the or
 iginal dual jobs regulations.The new field assistance bulletin clarifies t
 hat no current Wage and Hour Division guidance relies on the former percen
 tage-based limit on the time a tipped employee may spend performing relate
 d\, non-tipped duties\, commonly known as the 80/20 rule. The division rem
 oved its 80/20 rule guidance from its Field Operations Handbook in Januar
 y 2025\, and the handbook is now consistent with the reinstated 1967 dual 
 jobs regulations.As noted in the most recent regulatory agenda\, the depa
 rtment is developing a notice of proposed rulemaking to amend various regu
 lations for tipped employees under the FLSA. The department welcomes publi
 c comments on the proposed rule once it is published.Learn more about the 
 Wage and Hour Division\, including the agen\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/
 releases/whd/whd20261019-0
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261019-0
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0d6172f74edfa978@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261016T210457Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor awards $81.5M in grant
 s to support industry-driven job training programs in rural communities
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today announced th
 e award of $81.5 million in funding to address the critical workforce nee
 ds of employers in high-growth and emerging industries across the Appalach
 ian\, Delta\, and Northern Border regions. Administered by the departmen
 t’s Employment and Training Administration\, the seventh round of Work
 force Opportunity for Rural Communities program funding supports 13 projec
 ts across 11 states to stimulate employment and wage growth\, as well as e
 conomic mobility\, self-sufficiency\, and increased labor force participat
 ion. The demonstration grants support the workforce needs of regional empl
 oyers in predominantly rural areas to enable them to better compete econom
 ically and enable residents of the regions to obtain or retain family-sust
 aining employment opportunities so they can remain and thrive in their com
 munities.“These grants ensure those in our rural communities have a path
 way to the training they need for in-demand jobs that feed a family and st
 rengthen our supply chains\,” said Acting Secretary of Labor Keith Sonde
 rling. “They help us advance President Trump’s goal of building the wo
 rkforce for the Golden Age and enable more people to pursue the American D
 ream.”The WORC Initiative is made available in partnership with the App
 alachian Regional Commission\, the Delta Regional Authority\, and the N
 orthern Border Regional Commission.The grants will help provide industry-d
 riven training\, including Registered Apprenticeships\, for new and dislo
 cated workers\, and those already in the workforce who need to learn new 
 skills. These programs will focus on high-growth and emerging industry se
 ctors such as skilled trades and construction\, healthcare\, information t
 echnology\, manufacturing\, domestic energy production\, and business and 
 professional services. Grants are being awarded to recipients as follows:
 RecipientStateAmountTuskegee UniversityAL$7\,500\,000Arkansas Rural Health
  Partnership Inc.AR$6\,133\,431Eastern Kentucky Concentrated E\nhttp://www
 .dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261016
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261016
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
